 "verdicts": {
  "cpu_integer_hybrid": "integer GEMV exists in fused.py (numba Q8 dot, gated K>=512) but it is SCALAR integer code: measured slower than fp32 BLAS GEMV (gate_G3). The instruction-level dotprod path is 5.85x per MAC (gate_G1) and a C dotprod int8 GEMV is 4.9x the fp32 BLAS GEMV on the real decode shape; a scalar int8 kernel does not.",
  "device_hybrid_npu": "vendor QNN/HTP and Adreno OpenCL libraries exist on this device, but no /dev accelerator nodes are visible from proot and dlopen of all three fails on Android-side dependencies (liblog.so, libcutils.so, Android libc). NPU use requires an Android-native process + a QNN int8 graph: compatible in principle, not reachable from this shell.",
  "decode_speed": "decode is bandwidth-bound (gate_G4): what matters is weight BYTES, not FLOPS. Measured: fp32 4 bytes/param vs int8 1 vs 4-bit 0.5. A dotprod int8 GEMV gets ~2.5x over the fp32 dequant path; saturating the 4-bit memory floor needs a MAC rate well above the scalar dotprod kernel (i8mm/SVE2 or NPU).",
  "prefill_speed": "prefill is compute-bound (gate_G5): an int8 GEMM pays here, but only a kernel faster than fp32 BLAS (numba scalar int8 does not, gate_G2)."
 },
